#, kde-format
msgctxt "@title:window"
msgid "Welcome to Sparky Stereo OS"
msgstr ""

#, kde-kuit-format
msgctxt "@info:usagetip"
msgid ""
"Your whole desktop in 3D, on the 3D screen you already have: a 3D TV or "
"projector, a VR headset, a glasses-free 3D display, or any monitor with red-"
"cyan glasses. On a 2D monitor it is the desktop you know.<nl/><nl/>Each "
"screen is turned to 3D in System Settings, Display Configuration.<nl/><nl/"
">Sparky Stereo OS is Daniel Ramos's edition of SparkyLinux (by Paweł "
"“pavroo” Pijanowski), with KDE Plasma."
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@action:button"
msgid "Open Display Configuration"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@info:accessible"
msgid ""
"A 3D TV, a 3D monitor with its glasses, a monitor with red-cyan glasses and "
"a VR headset"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@title:window"
msgid "Point, Then Type"
msgstr ""

#, kde-kuit-format
msgctxt "@info:usagetip"
msgid ""
"The window under the pointer is the one that gets your keys. Move the "
"pointer onto a window and type: there is nothing to click. Leave the pointer "
"there for a moment and the window comes to the front.<nl/><nl/>Why: fewer "
"clicks and faster workflows, and fewer health problems as a result. Your "
"mouse lasts longer too.<nl/><nl/>If what you type does not appear, look "
"where the pointer is. To work another way, open Window Behavior in System "
"Settings and choose how a window gets the focus."
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@action:button"
msgid "Open Window Behavior"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@info:accessible"
msgid ""
"Two windows; the pointer rests on the front one, and the words typed appear "
"there"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@info drawn as words typed in a window"
msgid "Hello"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@title:window"
msgid "Touch and Tablet Mode"
msgstr ""

#, kde-kuit-format
msgctxt "@info:usagetip"
msgid ""
"On a touch screen a tap opens a file or folder in Dolphin and on the "
"desktop, while the mouse keeps its own setting: a double click, or one click "
"if you choose that in General Behavior.<nl/><nl/>On a computer with a touch "
"screen of its own, the on-screen keyboard is Plasma Keyboard, in your "
"language. It opens when you touch a text field in tablet mode, and in tablet "
"mode the panels hide by themselves and come back as they were when you leave "
"it."
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@action:button"
msgid "Open General Behavior"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@action:button"
msgid "Open Virtual Keyboard"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@title:window"
msgid "Your 3D Depth, Set Once"
msgstr ""

#, kde-kuit-format
msgctxt "@info:usagetip"
msgid ""
"How far the desktop reaches behind the screen and how far it comes out in "
"front of it is your choice, made in one place: the 3D Depth page in System "
"Settings.<nl/><nl/>Everything on the desktop stays inside those limits. They "
"are set for your screen and stay the same whatever the size of a window, a "
"picture or an icon, and whatever resolution a program draws at; no program "
"changes them.<nl/><nl/>Why: how much depth is comfortable is a matter of "
"taste, so you set it once and the whole desktop follows.<nl/><nl/>On a 2D "
"screen the desktop is flat and nothing changes. Your limits are kept for a "
"3D screen: they apply when one is connected, or when a screen is switched to "
"one of its 3D modes."
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@action:button"
msgid "Open 3D Depth"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@info:accessible"
msgid ""
"The desktop in depth: the wallpaper behind the screen, a window at the "
"screen, its menu in front of it"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@title:window"
msgid "A Wallpaper That Changes by Itself"
msgstr ""

#, kde-kuit-format
msgctxt "@info:usagetip"
msgid ""
"Stereo Variety changes the wallpaper by itself while it runs, every five "
"minutes. Open it with the button below: the first time, it greets you and "
"asks whether it may use the internet, and from then on it starts at every "
"login.<nl/><nl/>It shows the pictures of the sources ticked in its "
"Preferences. At first only folders on this computer are ticked; an online "
"source downloads only once you tick it. Variety does not tell a metered "
"connection from any other, so on a metered connection keep the online "
"sources unticked.<nl/><nl/>To keep the picture you have, untick “Change "
"wallpaper every” in its Preferences. To stop Variety, untick “Start Variety "
"when the computer starts” there too: it does not start again at the next "
"login."
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@action:button"
msgid "Open Variety's Preferences"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@title:window"
msgid "Keys and Sessions You Know from Windows"
msgstr ""

#, kde-kuit-format
msgctxt "@info:usagetip"
msgid ""
"Ctrl+Alt+Del opens System Monitor, where you see what is running and can end "
"a program that does not answer, as the Task Manager does on Windows; "
"Ctrl+Shift+Esc and Ctrl+Esc open it too. Ctrl+Alt+Esc shows the logout "
"screen, to log out, restart or shut down.<nl/><nl/>Each login starts with an "
"empty desktop: the programs that were open when you logged out do not come "
"back by themselves. Logging out, restarting and shutting down do not ask you "
"to confirm.<nl/><nl/>Why: your hands already know these keys, and a fresh "
"desktop at each login means a program that went wrong does not come back "
"with it.<nl/><nl/>To change a key, open Shortcuts. To have the programs of "
"your last session come back, or to be asked before logging out, open Desktop "
"Session."
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@action:button"
msgid "Open Shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#, kde-format
msgctxt "@action:button"
msgid "Open Desktop Session"
msgstr ""

